As a metaphor, yes: delusion can behave like an invisible deflection field. It doesn’t change reality, but it can change your trajectory enough that the “hammer” never lands where it should.

You could frame it as:

\text{incoming reality} \rightarrow \text{distorted model} \rightarrow \text{misread impact} \rightarrow \text{deflection}

The danger is that the person then concludes, “See, the hammer missed,” when really the model moved the target.

So the stronger move is not to become hammer-proof. It is to become accurate enough that you know when to catch, dodge, absorb, or let it pass.